## How to Decide Whether In-Person Therapy Is Right for You

You do not have to make the decision based solely on what other people recommend.

Try asking yourself:

**Where can I speak most openly?**

Would you feel more comfortable sitting in an office or talking from a private space at home?

**Which option is easier to maintain?**

Would traveling to an office fit realistically into your schedule?

**Where can I focus?**

Does your home provide enough privacy and separation from everyday responsibilities?

**What kind of connection feels natural to me?**

Some people enjoy face-to-face interaction, while others feel completely comfortable communicating through video.

**What does my therapist recommend?**

Depending on your circumstances, a mental health professional may be able to help you determine which format makes the most sense.

## Common Misconceptions About In-Person Therapy

### "In-person therapy is always more effective."

There is not enough evidence to make that universal claim. Studies comparing telehealth and face-to-face psychotherapy have found comparable outcomes across many of the conditions and settings studied.

### "I need to know exactly what to say before my appointment."

You don't.

It is normal to arrive unsure where to begin. Your therapist can ask questions and help organize the conversation.

### "I should feel comfortable immediately."

Not necessarily.

Therapy can involve discussing personal subjects, and building trust may take time. What matters is whether you gradually develop a respectful and collaborative working relationship.

### "Going to therapy means I am weak."

Seeking professional support is not a measure of weakness.

People seek counseling for many different reasons, including stress, relationships, grief, transitions, anxiety, depression, and personal challenges.

## When Should You Consider Professional Mental Health Support?

Consider speaking with a qualified mental health professional when emotional, behavioral, or relationship concerns are becoming difficult to manage on your own or are interfering with areas of your life.

You do not have to wait for a situation to become a crisis before seeking support.

A therapist can help you understand what you are experiencing, identify goals, and determine whether counseling may be appropriate.

If you are experiencing an immediate mental health emergency or believe you may be in danger, seek emergency or crisis assistance rather than relying on information in this article.

**Disclaimer:** This article is for general educational purposes and does not provide a diagnosis or individualized medical or mental health advice. A qualified professional can help determine what type of support is appropriate for your circumstances.

